<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>Born again: not a metaphor</h4>
<p>Many Protestant traditions emphasize being "born again" as a conscious adult decision. Help your students understand that in Catholic teaching, Baptism is the sacrament of being born again. For infants, the faith of the parents and godparents brings the child to Christ; the child will later profess that faith personally. Adults who convert are born again through Baptism. The key is that this is a real, sacramental rebirth, not just a spiritual experience.</p>

<h4>Original sin</h4>
<p>Many people misunderstand original sin. It is not personal sin, not a stain, not something the child did. It is the state of being born without sanctifying grace, with a wounded nature, outside of friendship with God. Baptism removes this state and restores the life of grace. Be careful to explain this positively: Baptism makes us children of God, not just "not sinners."</p>

<h4>Infant Baptism</h4>
<p>Be ready to explain why the Church baptizes infants. The answer is grace: just as Israelite babies received the covenant through circumcision before they could choose, Christian babies receive the New Covenant through Baptism. The child will later ratify this choice personally through Confirmation and First Communion. Grace precedes faith; it makes faith possible.</p>

<h4>The indelible character</h4>
<p>Explain that Baptism can never be repeated. The mark it imprints on the soul is permanent. Even if someone falls away from the Church, they remain baptized. This speaks to God's faithfulness: once he calls someone his own, he never goes back on that. It also means that even the greatest sinner can return to the sacraments through Reconciliation.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>🌱 Seed (ages 7-12)</h4>
<p>Focus on the images: being born again, washing clean, entering the family of God. Use pictures or even water and a shell to demonstrate baptismal action. Ask them to find out about their own Baptism. Activities: draw the Flood, the Red Sea, or Jesus in the Jordan.</p>

<h4>🌿 Sprouts (ages 13-17)</h4>
<p>They may struggle with infant Baptism: "How can a baby choose?" Help them see grace as gift, not reward. Connect with identity: "Who am I?" Baptism answers: "You are a child of God." Use the image of adoption: no one earns their place in a family; they are given it.</p>

<h4>🪴 Root (adults new to the faith)</h4>
<p>Many adult catechumens come from backgrounds where baptism was just a symbol. Emphasize that it is a real transformation: sins forgiven, new life given, identity changed. They may be nervous about Baptism; reassure them that God is doing the work, not them.</p>

<h4>🌳 Tree (formed adults)</h4>
<p>Go deeper into the theology: the relationship between Baptism and the Paschal Mystery (Rom 6), the connection to the Trinity (baptism in the name of Father, Son, and Spirit), and the ecclesial dimension (incorporation into the Body of Christ). You can quote St. Augustine or St. Thomas Aquinas.</p>

</section>

