<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<h4><em>&laquo;Why does the Church baptize babies? They can't choose!&raquo;</em></h4>
<p>Baptism is not primarily about our choice; it is about God's grace. Just as babies were circumcised in the Old Covenant before they could choose, babies are baptized in the New Covenant. The faith of the parents and godparents brings the child to Christ. The child will later ratify this faith personally at Confirmation. Salvation is God's gift, not our achievement.</p>

<h4><em>&laquo;What about people who are never baptized? Are they all damned?&raquo;</em></h4>
<p>The Church teaches that Baptism is necessary for salvation for those who have received the Gospel (CCC 1257). However, God is not bound by the sacraments. Those who, through no fault of their own, do not know Christ or his Church but seek God sincerely and strive to do his will may achieve salvation (CCC 1260). The Church trusts in God's mercy while remaining faithful to Christ's command.</p>

<h4><em>&laquo;I was baptized as a baby, but I don't feel like a Christian. What should I do?&raquo;</em></h4>
<p>The grace of Baptism is real whether you feel it or not. You are a child of God whether you claim that identity or not. The question is: will you live from that grace? Begin by learning more about your faith, go to Mass, talk to a priest. You don't need to be re-baptized; you need to be reawakened to what you already have.</p>

<h4><em>&laquo;Why can't Baptism be repeated? What if I want a fresh start?&raquo;</em></h4>
<p>Baptism imprints a permanent mark on the soul. God is faithful; he doesn't take back what he gives. If you need a fresh start, that is what the sacrament of Reconciliation (Penance) is for. You can renew your baptismal promises, but you cannot be baptized again. The indelible character is a gift, not a limitation.</p>

</section>

